A DOM event travels through capturing ancestors toward the target, reaches the target, and then normally bubbles back through ancestors. `addEventListener` uses bubbling by default; `{ capture: true }` observes the capture phase. Event delegation attaches one handler to a stable ancestor and inspects `event.target` or `closest()` to handle matching descendants. It reduces listener count and also works for descendants added later. The handler must account for non-matching targets, nested matching elements, and controls whose default action may need `preventDefault()`. Use `stopPropagation()` sparingly: it prevents other ancestors from observing the event and can make components harder to compose.

```ts caption="Delegate clicks from a stable list container and guard the event target."
list.addEventListener('click', (event) => {
  if (!(event.target instanceof Element)) return;
  const button = event.target.closest<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-id]');
  if (!button || !list.contains(button)) return;
  const id = button.dataset.id;
  if (id) selectItem(id);
});
```

`fetch()` rejects for failures such as a network error or an aborted request; an HTTP 404 or 500 still produces a fulfilled `Response`. Therefore check `response.ok` or `response.status` before parsing or returning application data. Pass an `AbortSignal` from an `AbortController` to cancel work when a component, route, or request becomes irrelevant; handle the resulting abort separately from a genuine failure. Also verify the response format and avoid reading a response body more than once. A robust wrapper defines timeout/cancellation policy, maps expected HTTP statuses to domain results, and preserves enough context for logging without exposing sensitive request data.

```ts caption="Treat HTTP failure statuses separately from fetch rejection."
const controller = new AbortController();
const response = await fetch('/api/items', { signal: controller.signal });
if (!response.ok) throw new Error(`HTTP ${response.status}`);
const items = await response.json();
// controller.abort() when the operation is no longer needed
```

Use a dedicated worker for CPU-heavy work that would otherwise block the main thread, such as parsing or computation. A worker has its own global context and cannot directly manipulate the DOM. The main thread and worker communicate with `postMessage()` and `message` events using the structured clone algorithm; data is copied unless a transferable object is deliberately transferred. That boundary adds serialization cost and requires an explicit protocol for request IDs, errors, cancellation, and worker shutdown. Keep UI work on the main thread, batch messages, and measure whether the computation is large enough to justify worker startup and communication overhead. Terminate workers that are no longer needed.

```ts caption="A worker keeps expensive parsing away from the UI thread."
// main.ts
const worker = new Worker(new URL('./parse.worker.ts', import.meta.url), { type: 'module' });
worker.onmessage = ({ data }: MessageEvent<{ rows: string[][] }>) => render(data.rows);
worker.postMessage({ text });
// worker: self.onmessage = ({ data }) => self.postMessage({ rows: parse(data.text) });
```

Use `MutationObserver` to react to changes in a DOM subtree, attributes, or character data; it replaces deprecated mutation events. Use `ResizeObserver` when an element's box changes, especially for component layout that is not equivalent to viewport resizing. Use `IntersectionObserver` when you need to know whether a target intersects a root or viewport, such as lazy loading or visibility analytics. Observers deliver asynchronous callbacks, so code should tolerate batches and inspect the supplied records/entries rather than assume one callback per change. Disconnect them when the observed component is destroyed, avoid changing the observed condition recursively without a guard, and use thresholds or filters to limit work.

```ts caption="Observe visibility without polling scroll events."
const observer = new IntersectionObserver((entries) => {
  for (const entry of entries) {
    if (entry.isIntersecting) loadImage(entry.target as HTMLImageElement);
  }
}, { rootMargin: '200px' });
observer.observe(image);
// observer.disconnect() during teardown
```

A service worker is registered for an origin and scope, then installed, activated, and used to handle functional events such as fetch. A newly installed worker can wait while an older worker controls open pages; activation is where old caches and other versioned resources are commonly cleaned. `event.waitUntil()` keeps installation or activation work alive until its promise settles. The worker has no DOM and must use asynchronous APIs. HTTPS is required, with localhost treated as secure for development. Main hazards are stale assets, unbounded caches, inconsistent versions across clients, and assuming registration means the current page is already controlled. Version caches deliberately and test update, rollback, and offline failure paths.

```ts caption="Clean versioned caches during activation."
self.addEventListener('activate', (event) => {
  event.waitUntil((async () => {
    const keep = 'assets-v2';
    for (const key of await caches.keys()) {
      if (key !== keep) await caches.delete(key);
    }
  })());
});
```

Storage capacity is origin- and browser-dependent, so do not build correctness around a remembered fixed number. `navigator.storage.estimate()` provides approximate `usage` and `quota`; writes can still fail, so catch quota errors and make data recoverable or sync it to a server. By default, site storage is best-effort and may be evicted, including IndexedDB and Cache API data. `navigator.storage.persist()` can request persistent storage for critical data, and `persisted()` reports its state, but users can always clear data. Budget caches, delete obsolete entries, separate reproducible assets from user data, and expose a degraded/offline recovery path rather than treating local storage as the source of truth.

```ts caption="Treat quota estimates as guidance and writes as fallible."
const estimate = await navigator.storage.estimate();
const used = estimate.usage ?? 0;
const quota = estimate.quota ?? 0;
if (quota && used / quota > 0.8) await pruneOldCaches();
try { await saveOfflineData(record); } catch (error) {
  // Surface a recovery/sync path for quota failures.
  reportStorageFailure(error);
}
```

The Permissions API gives a consistent way to query the current permission state for supported capabilities in the current context. `navigator.permissions.query({ name })` returns a `PermissionStatus` whose state is typically `granted`, `denied`, or `prompt`, and whose `change` event can signal later changes. It is a status query, not a universal grant mechanism: the capability may still require a user-gesture API call, may not support Permissions API querying, or may have browser-specific policy. Ask only when the feature is useful, explain why, handle denial and dismissal, and re-check state when the user returns or settings may have changed. Never assume a previous grant is permanent.

```ts caption="Observe permission state, then request access in the feature flow."
const status = await navigator.permissions.query({ name: 'geolocation' });
const update = () => {
  button.disabled = status.state === 'denied';
};
status.addEventListener('change', update);
update();
```

Open the database with an explicit version and perform schema changes in `onupgradeneeded`, where the version-change transaction can create or modify object stores and indexes. Keep migrations deterministic, small, and backward-aware: existing records may need transformation, and a failed upgrade must not leave the application pretending it has the new schema. Multiple tabs can keep an old connection open, so listen for `versionchange`, close the old connection, and coordinate the user experience for reopening. Treat IndexedDB as asynchronous and fallible; handle blocked, error, and quota cases. Roll out migrations with observability and a recovery or reset plan for incompatible local data.

```ts caption="Perform versioned schema work in the upgrade transaction."
const request = indexedDB.open('app', 2);
request.onupgradeneeded = () => {
  const db = request.result;
  if (!db.objectStoreNames.contains('notes')) {
    db.createObjectStore('notes', { keyPath: 'id' });
  }
};
request.onblocked = () => showCloseOtherTabsMessage();
```

Start by separating authoritative server state, durable client state, and disposable caches. Use IndexedDB or Cache Storage for asynchronous durable data accessible from worker contexts; avoid using `localStorage` as a cross-context database because Web Storage is synchronous and unavailable in service workers. For lightweight tab notifications, the `storage` event or `BroadcastChannel` can signal that state changed, but messages are not a replacement for conflict resolution or durable delivery. Workers communicate through message channels and structured cloning. Define versioned messages, idempotent commands, ordering assumptions, and recovery after a worker restart. Re-read authoritative records after notifications, handle concurrent writes, and test crashes, quota failures, stale tabs, and offline reconciliation.

```ts caption="Broadcast an invalidation signal; reload durable state from IndexedDB."
const channel = new BroadcastChannel('sync');
channel.onmessage = async ({ data }) => {
  if (data.type === 'note-changed') await refreshNoteFromIndexedDB(data.id);
};
channel.postMessage({ type: 'note-changed', id });
// channel.close() during teardown
```
